<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Production policy: TLS only, anchored exclusively in Android's system CA store. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!--
      Certificate pinning for SecureChat's own hosts.

      Without it the app accepts any of the ~150 CAs Android ships with, so a single compromised or
      mis-issuing CA is enough to intercept logins, tokens and metadata. Pinning narrows that to
      Let's Encrypt alone.

      What is pinned, and why not the server certificate: the leaf renews every 60 days and gets a
      new key each time, and this APK is installed by hand over USB. Pinning it would take every
      phone offline on each renewal until someone walked round with a cable. The two pins below are
      the ISRG roots the live chain actually terminates in, both valid until September 2032, so
      renewals do not touch them.

      Computed from the real chain served by chat.securechat.com.au on 2026-09-01, not copied from
      documentation:
        openssl s_client -showcerts -connect chat.securechat.com.au:443 \
          -servername chat.securechat.com.au </dev/null
        # then for each certificate:
        openssl x509 -noout -pubkey | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der \
          | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | base64

      What this does NOT stop: anyone who can pass Let's Encrypt's domain validation - by taking the
      server, the DNS, or the route - still gets a certificate this pin accepts. Closing that needs
      pinning our own key, which brings back the renewal risk above.

      expiration is a deliberate safety valve, not an oversight. After that date Android ignores
      these pins instead of refusing to connect, so a forgotten APK update degrades to "unpinned"
      rather than to a fleet of phones that cannot reach the server. Re-check the pins and move the
      date forward well before it passes; see docs/TLS-PINNING.md.
    -->
    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">chat.securechat.com.au</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">push.securechat.com.au</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2027-09-01">
            <!-- ISRG Root X2 - the anchor of the current chain. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">diGVwiVYbubAI3RW4hB9xU8e/CH2GnkuvVFZE8zmgzI=</pin>
            <!-- ISRG Root YE - one level down, kept as the backup pin so a single change at the
                 top of Let's Encrypt's hierarchy cannot lock the fleet out. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">sCkq5UWXjg+7mKu9lMhhYF5bGLsy7VI/UNW3tccdR7w=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
